{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L1y5rv2T9bka4qMD6XomPQ","type":"tool_result","content":"404\t### 2.12 Stored credential files\n405\t\n406\t**Paths.**\n407\t\n408\t- `projectCredentialsPath(dir)`: `<stateDir(dir)>/credentials.json`.\n409\t  Error: `project credentials: not inside a git repository`.\n410\t- `userCredentialsPath(env)`: if `env.XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is non-empty,\n411\t  `<XDG_CONFIG_HOME>/roast/credentials.json` (used as given, even if\n412\t  relative); else if `env.HOME` is non-empty,\n413\t  `<HOME>/.config/roast/credentials.json`; else error\n414\t  `user credentials: $HOME is not defined`. Read `HOME` from the injected\n415\t  environment, not `os.homedir()` (which falls back to the passwd entry\n416\t  and would defeat test isolation).\n417\t\n418\t**File shape (version 1, unchanged from Go):**\n419\t\n420\t```json\n421\t{\n422\t  \"version\": 1,\n423\t  \"providers\": {\n424\t    \"anthropic\": {\n425\t      \"key\": \"sk-ant-...\",\n426\t      \"stored_at\": \"2026-11-14T12:00:00Z\"\n427\t    },\n428\t    \"openai\": {\n429\t      \"key\": \"sk-...\",\n430\t      \"stored_at\": \"2026-11-14T12:00:00.123Z\"\n431\t    }\n432\t  }\n433\t}\n434\t```\n435\t\n436\t- Written with 2-space indentation, top-level order `version`,\n437\t  `providers`; provider names sorted ascending; entry order `key`,\n438\t  `stored_at`; trailing newline. An empty store writes `\"providers\": {}`.\n439\t- `stored_at` is written in UTC as RFC 3339 with `Z`, fractional seconds\n440\t  present only when non-zero and with trailing zeros trimmed (Go's\n441\t  `RFC3339Nano` form). JavaScript `Date` has millisecond precision, so at\n442\t  most three fractional digits are produced; an existing entry's\n443\t  `stored_at` string is preserved verbatim when the file is rewritten for\n444\t  another provider.\n445\t\n446\t**Loading** (`loadCredentials(path)` -> `Map<provider, Entry>`):\n447\t\n448\t1. Open the file. `ENOENT` -> empty map (a missing file holds no\n449\t   credentials). Other open error -> `read credentials: <cause>`.\n450\t2. `fstat` the open descriptor (follows symlinks, like Go). If\n451\t   `mode & 0o077 !== 0` (any group or other bit, not only read) -> error\n452\t   `credentials file <path> is readable by other users (mode <perm>); run\n453\t   `chmod 600 <path>``. Never skipped, never auto-fixed.\n454\t3. Read the contents and decode. Decode errors are reported as\n455\t   `credentials file <path>: <cause>`. Decoding rejects:\n456\t   - not JSON, or trailing data after the top-level value;\n457\t   - any unknown member at the top level or inside an entry;\n458\t   - `version` missing or not the integer 1\n459\t     (`unsupported version <v> (want 1)`);\n460\t   - a provider name that `lookupProvider` does not know\n461\t     (`unknown provider \"<name>\"`);\n462\t   - an entry whose `key` is missing, not a string, or empty after\n463\t     trimming whitespace (`provider \"<name>\" has an empty key`);\n464\t   - `stored_at` present but not an RFC 3339 timestamp string.\n465\t   Accepted: `providers` missing or `null` (empty map); `stored_at`\n466\t   missing or `null` (treated as Go's zero time, written back as\n467\t   `0001-01-01T00:00:00Z` if the file is rewritten).\n468\t4. Load errors are not cached (2.13).\n469\t\n470\t**Saving** (`saveCredential(path, provider, key, storedAt)`):\n471\t\n472\t1. `provider` unknown -> `save credential: unknown provider \"<name>\"`.\n473\t2. `key.trim() === \"\"` -> `save credential: empty key`.\n474\t3. Load the existing file (2.12 Loading); any error aborts (so an\n475\t   over-permissive or malformed file blocks saving).\n476\t4. Set `providers[provider] = {key, stored_at: storedAt in UTC}`, replacing\n477\t   any previous entry. The key is stored as given (not trimmed).\n478\t5. Write atomically (below).\n479\t\n480\t**Deleting** (`deleteCredential(path, provider)`): load (errors abort); if\n481\tthe provider has no entry, or the file is missing, do nothing (no write);\n482\telse remove it and write atomically. The file remains with the other\n483\tproviders, or with an empty `providers` object.\n484\t\n485\t**Atomic write** for credentials:\n486\t\n487\t1. `mkdir -p` the parent with mode `0o700`.\n488\t2. `chmod` the parent to `0o700` even if it already existed (tightens a\n489\t   `0o755` `.git/roast` created by the trust record, or a loose\n490\t   `~/.config/roast`).\n491\t3. Create `credentials-<random>.json` in the parent with exclusive create\n492\t   and mode `0o600`, write the bytes, close.\n493\t4. Rename over `credentials.json`. Remove the temp file on any failure.\n494\t5. Afterwards the directory holds only `credentials.json` (plus whatever\n495\t   else was there before, such as `trust.json`); no temp file survives.\n496\t6. Error prefix: `write credentials: <cause>`.\n497\t\n498\tNo `fsync` (Go parity).\n499\t\n500\t### 2.13 Credential store (the `StoredCredentials` implementation)\n501\t\n502\tConfig consumes stored credentials only through:\n503\t\n504\t```ts\n505\ttype CredentialScope = 'project'|'user'\n506\tinterface StoredCredentials {\n507\t    // Returns null when neither scope holds a key for provider.\n508\t    // Rejects when a credentials file it reaches cannot be used;\n509\t    // never skips a bad file.\n510\t    lookup(provider:string):Promise<{key:string, scope:CredentialScope}|null>\n511\t}\n512\t```\n513\t\n514\t`CredentialStore(dir, env)` implements it:\n515\t\n516\t- Lookup order: project scope, then user scope. The first scope whose map\n517\t  has the provider wins, returning its key and scope name.\n518\t- Files are read lazily: the project file is read on the first lookup;\n519\t  the user file only when a lookup gets past the project scope without a\n520\t  match. Each file is read at most once per store after a successful load\n521\t  (cache the loaded map, or an in-flight promise, per scope; on failure\n522\t  drop the cache entry so the next lookup retries and fails again).\n523\t- An error loading the project file rejects the lookup even if the user\n524\t  file holds the key.\n525\t- `dir === \"\"`: no project scope. `projectCredentialsPath` failing (not a\n526\t  repository): no project scope, cached. `userCredentialsPath` failing (no\n527\t  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and no `HOME`): no user scope, cached.\n528\t- The CLI constructs one store per command run with the working directory\n529\t  and injected environment, and attaches it to every config it loads\n530\t  (`withStoredCredentials`, config area). Any path that resolves roles from\n531\t  a loaded config must attach it, or stored keys are silently unused.\n532\t\n533\tHow config uses it (restated from the config area for context): a stored\n534\tkey is consulted only after `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`, the profile's\n535\t`api_key_env`, and the provider's standard variable; only when\n536\t`providerAt(role endpoint, role protocol)` finds a provider; and the\n537\tprovider name passed to `lookup` comes from that registry match, never\n538\tfrom anything in the project config. A rejected lookup fails role\n539\tresolution, which `review` reports as a usage error (exit 2) before any\n540\tnetwork request.\n541\t\n542\t### 2.14 Doctor (reference only; doctor area owns it)\n543\t\n544\t- `config-trust` check: pass `no approval required ...` when\n545\t  `!requiresApproval`; fail when outside a Git repository, when the record\n546\t  cannot be read (`approval required; could not read approvals: <err>`),\n547\t  or when not approved (`... endpoint probes skipped; run `npx roast\n548\t  trust``); pass when approved. In TS, a `'legacy'` (version 1) record is a\n549\t  fail whose detail says `approved by an older Roast` and names\n550\t  `npx roast trust`. Doctor never prompts and never approves.\n551\t- When not trusted, doctor skips the endpoint and round-trip probes for\n552\t  every role (the probes would send credentials to an unapproved\n553\t  destination).\n554\t- `project-credentials` / `user-credentials` checks: for each scope whose\n555\t  path resolves and whose file exists (`lstat`), `loadCredentials`; fail\n556\t  with the load error, else pass with `<path> (<sorted providers>)` or\n557\t  `<path> (none stored)`. A broken file fails doctor even when environment\n558\t  credentials shadow it.\n559\t\n560\t### 2.15 Security invariants enforced here\n561\t\n562\tFrom `docs/security-model.md`, ADR-0001, ADR-0002:\n563\t\n564\t1. No network request is made for a config that requires approval until\n565\t   this clone has approved its exact projection hash (review fails closed;\n566\t   doctor skips probes).\n567\t2. Approval state lives only under the Git common directory, which `git\n568\t   clone` never copies; a repository cannot ship its own approval.\n569\t3. Only `y` / `yes` records approval; `--yes` is the only non-interactive\n570\t   path. There is no environment-variable bypass.\n571\t4. Every value shown for approval is escaped, so config text cannot alter\n572\t   what the user is asked to approve.\n573\t5. Any change to an endpoint, protocol, credential variable, or\n574\t   prompt-override path invalidates the approval; other edits do not.\n575\t6. Approvals recorded by the Go implementation are never honored.\n576\t7. A malformed or unsupported trust record is an error, never read as\n577\t   \"approved\" or silently replaced.\n578\t8. A project config never holds a credential (config area rejects\n579\t   `api_key`); stored keys come only from credentials files outside the\n580\t   working tree.\n581\t9. A stored key is matched to a role only through the built-in provider\n582\t   registry (endpoint plus accepted protocol), so a custom endpoint, or a\n583\t   role an override moved off its provider's endpoint, never receives one.\n584\t10. Stored keys rank below every environment variable.\n585\t11. Credentials files are created `0o600` in a `0o700` directory; a file\n586\t    with any group/other permission bit stops resolution with an error\n587\t    naming `chmod 600`, never skipped.\n588\t12. A malformed credentials file (unknown provider or field, wrong\n589\t    version, empty key) is an error, never skipped.\n590\t13. Stored keys never appear in stdout, stderr, debug output, or run\n591\t    records (record area enforces exclusion from metadata; this area never\n592\t    logs a key or includes it in an error message).\n593\t\n594\t## 3. TypeScript module design\n595\t\n596\t```\n597\tsrc/config/trust.ts       projection, requiresApproval, hash v2\n598\tsrc/config/canonical.ts   RFC 8785 serializer for string-only documents\n599\tsrc/trust/trust.ts        stateDir, trustPath, record read/approve\n600\tsrc/credentials/credentials.ts\n601\t                          paths, load/save/delete, CredentialStore\n602\tsrc/fsutil/atomic.ts      writeFileAtomic (shared with trust; other areas\n603\t                          may reuse)\n604\tsrc/cli/trust.ts          `trust` command, requireTrust, summary, prompt\n605\tsrc/cli/prompt.ts         interactiveTerminals, line reader (shared)\n606\t```\n607\t\n608\t`src/config/trust.ts` (pure, no I/O):\n609\t\n610\t```ts\n611\texport interface TrustedProfile { /* 2.2 */ }\n612\texport interface PromptOverride { /* 2.2 */ }\n613\texport interface TrustProjection { /* 2.2 */ }\n614\texport function trustProjection(cfg:Config):TrustProjection\n615\texport function requiresApproval(p:TrustProjection):boolean\n616\texport function trustHashDocument(p:TrustProjection):HashDocument\n617\texport function trustHash(p:TrustProjection):string  // \"sha256:<hex>\"\n618\t```\n619\t\n620\t`trustHash` uses `node:crypto` `createHash('sha256')`. Depends on the\n621\tconfig area's `Config`, `providerAt`.\n622\t\n623\t`src/config/canonical.ts`:\n624\t\n625\t```ts\n626\texport type CanonicalValue =\n627\t    string|CanonicalValue[]|{[key:string]:CanonicalValue}\n628\texport function canonicalJson(value:CanonicalValue):string\n629\t```\n630\t\n631\tThrows on a string containing a lone surrogate (RFC 8785 requires\n632\twell-formed Unicode). This is a defensive backstop only: config\n633\tvalidation rejects lone surrogates (config area, spec.md cross-cutting\n634\trules), so hashing never sees one.\n635\t\n636\t`src/config/config.ts` (config area) exports the `StoredCredentials`\n637\tinterface (2.13) so config never imports `src/credentials/` (Go keeps the\n638\tsame direction: credentials imports config for `lookupProvider`).\n639\t\n640\t`src/trust/trust.ts`:\n641\t\n642\t```ts\n643\texport async function stateDir(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n644\t    :Promise<string>\n645\texport async function trustPath(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n646\t    :Promise<string>\n647\texport type TrustRecord =\n648\t    {kind:'current', approved:string[]}|{kind:'legacy'}\n649\texport async function readTrustRecord(path:string):Promise<TrustRecord>\n650\texport type ApprovalState = 'approved'|'not-approved'|'legacy'\n651\texport async function isApproved(dir:string, hash:string)\n652\t    :Promise<ApprovalState>\n653\texport async function approve(dir:string, hash:string):Promise<void>\n654\t```\n655\t\n656\tGit runs through `execFile('git', [...], {cwd:dir})` (Git area helper).\n657\t\n658\t`src/credentials/credentials.ts`:\n659\t\n660\t```ts\n661\texport const CREDENTIALS_FILE = 'credentials.json'\n662\texport type CredentialScope = 'project'|'user'\n663\texport interface Entry { key:string, storedAt:string }\n664\texport async function projectCredentialsPath(dir:string):Promise<string>\n665\texport function userCredentialsPath(\n666\t    env:Record<string, string|undefined>):string\n667\texport async function loadCredentials(path:string)\n668\t    :Promise<Map<string, Entry>>\n669\texport async function saveCredential(path:string, provider:string,\n670\t    key:string, storedAt:Date):Promise<void>\n671\texport async function deleteCredential(path:string, provider:string)\n672\t    :Promise<void>\n673\texport class CredentialStore implements StoredCredentials {\n674\t    constructor(dir:string, env:Record<string, string|undefined>)\n675\t    lookup(provider:string)\n676\t        :Promise<{key:string, scope:CredentialScope}|null>\n677\t}\n678\t```\n679\t\n680\t`storedAt` is kept as the original string so rewrites preserve it. The\n681\tdecoder is a `zod` strict schema plus the provider/key/version checks of\n682\t2.12. Depends on `src/config` (`lookupProvider`), `src/trust`\n683\t(`stateDir`), `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`.\n684\t\n685\t`src/fsutil/atomic.ts`:\n686\t\n687\t```ts\n688\texport async function writeFileAtomic(path:string, data:string, opts:{\n689\t    tempPrefix:string     // 'trust-' | 'credentials-'\n690\t    dirMode:number        // 0o755 | 0o700\n691\t    chmodDir:boolean      // false for trust, true for credentials\n692\t    errorPrefix:string    // 'write trust record' | 'write credentials'\n693\t}):Promise<void>\n694\t```\n695\t\n696\tTemp name: `<tempPrefix><random hex>.json`, opened with flags `wx` and\n697\tmode `0o600`.\n698\t\n699\t`src/cli/trust.ts`:\n700\t\n701\t```ts\n702\texport async function requireTrust(dir:string, cfg:Config,\n703\t    io:CliIo):Promise<void>        // throws UsageError or Error\n704\texport function writeTrustSummary(out:Writable,\n705\t    p:TrustProjection):void\n706\texport async function promptNoDefault(out:Writable,\n707\t    lines:LineReader, question:string):Promise<boolean>\n708\texport function trustCommand(...):CommandModule  // yargs\n709\t```\n710\t\n711\t`CliIo` is the in-process `run(argv, io)` bundle (stdin, stdout, stderr,\n712\tenv, cwd, shared line reader). `UsageError` maps to exit 2; other errors\n713\tto exit 4 (cli area). Debug namespace `roast:trust` and\n714\t`roast:credentials`: may log paths, scope names, provider names, record\n715\tversions, and hashes; never a key.\n716\t\n717\t## 4. Deliberate changes\n718\t\n719\t- **Trust hash v2** (decision 4, ADR-0003): SHA-256 over RFC 8785\n720\t  canonical JSON of the hash document in 2.4, `trust.json` `version: 2`.\n721\t  A version-1 record is never an approval: review fails as untrusted\n722\t  (exit 2, message says approved by an older Roast, names\n723\t  `npx roast trust`; on terminals the normal inline trust prompt is\n724\t  offered, as for any untrusted config); approving\n725\t  rewrites the file as version 2 with only the new hash. Differences from\n726\t  the Go hash document beyond the algorithm: empty arrays serialize as\n727\t  `[]` (Go wrote `null`), destination sort uses UTF-16 code units per\n728\t  field instead of bytes of NUL-joined fields. The stored form stays\n729\t  `sha256:<lowercase hex>`, and the test vectors in 2.4 are frozen.\n730\t- **Credentials keys are case-sensitive** (deliberate change 7): Go\n731\t  matched `credentials.json` field names case-insensitively\n732\t  (`{\"Version\":1,\"Providers\":...}` loaded); TS matches exactly, so such a\n733\t  file is rejected as an unknown member. Files Roast wrote are unaffected.\n734\t- **Windows dropped** (decision 2): the Go `runtime.GOOS != \"windows\"`\n735\t  guard on the permission check disappears; the check always runs.\n736\t- **Debug tracing** (decision 5): `roast:trust`, `roast:credentials`; no\n737\t  keys.\n738\t\n739\t## 5. Test plan\n740\t\n741\tSeams: CLI in-process (`run(argv, io)` with a `node:http` fake inference\n742\tserver and swappable `fetch`) unless noted. Every CLI test runs in a temp\n743\tGit repository and isolates `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in the injected\n744\tenv. Assert exit codes, files, and requests received; never exact text,\n745\texcept that a message names `npx roast trust` (behavioral contract).\n746\t\n747\t### 5.1 Projection and approval need (unit, `src/config/trust.ts`)\n748\t\n749\t- Table of single-profile configs and whether they need approval: OpenAI\n750\t  responses/chat with standard or no key var; implicit endpoint; implicit\n751\t  protocol; trailing slash; Anthropic; OpenRouter chat/responses with\n752\t  standard or no key; custom key var on each provider; another provider's\n753\t  key var; mismatched protocol; gateway with and without key var\n754\t  (TestStandardProfilesNeedNoApproval).\n755\t- Projection order: roles sorted, author routes sorted after their role;\n756\t  unselected profiles excluded; prompt overrides in fixed order (new).\n757\t\n758\t### 5.2 Hash v2 (unit)\n759\t\n760\t- Known-answer vectors 1 and 2 from 2.4 (new).\n761\t- Duplicate destinations collapse; destination order independent of role\n762\t  order; profile renames and selector changes keep the hash (new).\n763\t- `canonicalJson` matches RFC 8785 on key ordering and string escaping,\n764\t  including a non-ASCII key and a control character (new).\n765\t- Destinations sort field by field in UTF-16 code unit order (a case with\n766\t  an astral character versus a BMP character above U+E000); empty lists\n767\t  hash as `[]` (new).\n768\t- `canonicalJson` throws on a lone surrogate; the config area rejects one\n769\t  before hashing (new).\n770\t\n771\t### 5.3 Trust record (unit, `src/trust/trust.ts`, temp Git repo)\n772\t\n773\t- Absent file: not approved; approve writes version 2 with one hash, mode\n774\t  `0o600` (new).\n775\t- Approve twice: one entry (new).\n776\t- Version 1 file: `isApproved` returns `'legacy'`; approve replaces it\n777\t  with version 2 `[hash]` (new).\n778\t- Malformed JSON, `version: 3`, missing version, non-array `approved`:\n779\t  `isApproved` and `approve` both throw; file unchanged (new).\n780\t- Linked worktree resolves the same path (covered at CLI below).\n781\t\n782\t### 5.4 Review and the trust command (CLI)\n783\t\n784\t- Built-in-only config with author route reviews with no trust state\n785\t  written (TestDefaultEndpointConfigNeedsNoApproval).\n786\t- Untrusted config: exit neither 0 nor 1, message names `npx roast trust`,\n787\t  zero requests (TestUntrustedConfigFailsClosedBeforeAnyRequest).\n788\t- Summary names every profile, endpoint, protocol, credential var, prompt\n789\t  path, and asks `[y/N]` (TestTrustSummaryNamesEverySecurityRelevantValue;\n790\t  assert presence of config values only).\n791\t- Control characters in profile names, route families, and prompt paths\n792\t  never reach stdout raw; no config value starts its own line\n793\t  (TestTrustSummaryEscapesControlCharacters).\n794\t- `trust` records only on `y`/`Y`/`yes`/`y` without newline; `\"\"`, `\\n`,\n795\t  `n`, `no`, `yy`, `sure` exit non-zero, record nothing, and the next\n796\t  review still fails closed (TestTrustRecordsApprovalOnlyOnYes).\n797\t- `trust --yes` never reads stdin and enables the review\n798\t  (TestTrustYesRecordsWithoutReadingStdin).\n799\t- Model, threshold, limit, and gate edits keep approval\n800\t  (TestNonSecurityEditsKeepApproval).\n801\t- Endpoint, routed endpoint, protocol, credential var, and prompt path\n802\t  edits require re-approval, and re-approval works\n803\t  (TestSecurityEditsRequireApprovalAgain).\n804\t- Approval in the main worktree is honored in a linked worktree, and not\n805\t  before (TestApprovalIsSharedWithLinkedWorktrees).\n806\t- Inline prompt on simulated terminals: `y` approves, summary on stderr,\n807\t  record written, later non-terminal review passes\n808\t  (TestInlineTrustPromptApprovesOnYes).\n809\t- Inline decline (`\"\"`, `\\n`, `n`, `no`, `yy`): asks `[y/N]`, no requests,\n810\t  no record (TestInlineTrustPromptDeclinedSendsNothing).\n811\t- Non-terminal review checks for terminals, never reads stdin, never\n812\t  prompts, names `npx roast trust`\n813\t  (TestNonTerminalReviewFailsClosedWithoutPrompting).\n814\t- Default terminal detection rejects non-TTY streams\n815\t  (TestDefaultTerminalDetectionRejectsNonFiles; unit).\n816\t- Legacy record: review exits 2 with zero requests; `trust --yes` then\n817\t  makes it pass and the file is version 2; on simulated terminals `y`\n818\t  approves inline (new).\n819\t- Malformed record: review exits 4 with zero requests (new, Go parity;\n820\t  resolved in spec.md \"Kept as Go does\").\n821\t\n822\t### 5.5 Credentials (unit, `src/credentials/credentials.ts`)\n823\t\n824\t- Project path is `<git-common-dir>/roast/credentials.json`\n825\t  (TestProjectPathIsInsideTheGitCommonDir).\n826\t- User path follows `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, else `HOME/.config`\n827\t  (TestUserPathFollowsXDGConfigHome).\n828\t- Save writes `0o600` in `0o700`, replaces atomically, leaves only the\n829\t  file, round-trips keys and `stored_at`\n830\t  (TestSaveWritesAPrivateFileAndReplacesItAtomically).\n831\t- Save tightens an existing `0o755` directory\n832\t  (TestSaveTightensAnExistingDirectory).\n833\t- Delete removes one provider; deleting from a missing file is a no-op\n834\t  (TestDeleteRemovesOneProvider).\n835\t- Missing file loads empty (TestLoadMissingFileIsEmpty).\n836\t- Rejects unknown provider, unknown entry field, unknown top-level field,\n837\t  missing version, wrong version, empty key, non-JSON\n838\t  (TestLoadRejectsMalformedFiles).\n839\t- Member names match exactly: `{\"Version\":1,...}` and `\"Providers\"` or\n840\t  `\"Key\"` spellings are rejected as unknown members (new, deliberate\n841\t  change 7).\n842\t- Rejects modes `0o640`, `0o604`, `0o644`\n843\t  (TestLoadRejectsAFileOthersCanRead); add `0o620` (any group/other bit).\n844\t- Store prefers project over user scope\n845\t  (TestStorePrefersProjectScopeOverUserScope).\n846\t- Outside a repository only user scope is used\n847\t  (TestStoreOutsideARepositoryUsesOnlyUserScope).\n848\t- A bad file rejects the lookup (TestStoreReportsABadFile).\n849\t\n850\t### 5.6 Stored credentials through review and doctor (CLI)\n851\t\n852\t- Project stored key reaches the fake Anthropic endpoint\n853\t  (TestReviewSendsAProjectStoredKey).\n854\t- Env beats project beats user (TestEnvironmentBeatsProjectBeatsUserStoredKeys).\n855\t- Stored key never reaches a custom endpoint\n856\t  (TestStoredKeyNeverReachesACustomEndpoint).\n857\t- Stored key does not follow an endpoint override\n858\t  (TestStoredKeyDoesNotFollowAnEndpointOverride).\n859\t- An over-permissive file in either scope fails the review with zero\n860\t  requests (TestACredentialsFileOthersCanReadFailsTheReview).\n861\t- No copy of the stored key in stdout, stderr, or any run-record file\n862\t  (TestReviewLeavesNoCopyOfAStoredKey).\n863\t- Doctor reports a stored credential and its scope; fails a broken file;\n864\t  reports a usable file; still runs other checks with a broken file\n865\t  (TestDoctorReportsAStoredCredentialAndItsScope,\n866\t  TestDoctorFailsABrokenCredentialsFile,\n867\t  TestDoctorReportsAUsableCredentialsFile,\n868\t  TestDoctorRunsItsOtherChecksWithABrokenCredentialsFile; doctor area).\n869\t\n870\t## 6. Go baseline references\n871\t\n872\tAt commit `cd08f10`:\n873\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2b2ffa6-15d5-4c22-b042-75e0dc4ef2c9","uuid":"866b80e0-8c80-48cb-8bc7-f7dc27b209d3","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:38:21.182Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/roast.ts-rewrite-08-stored-credentials/.scratch/ts-rewrite/spec/03-trust-credentials.md","content":"### 2.12 Stored credential files\n\n**Paths.**\n\n- `projectCredentialsPath(dir)`: `<stateDir(dir)>/credentials.json`.\n  Error: `project credentials: not inside a git repository`.\n- `userCredentialsPath(env)`: if `env.XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is non-empty,\n  `<XDG_CONFIG_HOME>/roast/credentials.json` (used as given, even if\n  relative); else if `env.HOME` is non-empty,\n  `<HOME>/.config/roast/credentials.json`; else error\n  `user credentials: $HOME is not defined`. Read `HOME` from the injected\n  environment, not `os.homedir()` (which falls back to the passwd entry\n  and would defeat test isolation).\n\n**File shape (version 1, unchanged from Go):**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"providers\": {\n    \"anthropic\": {\n      \"key\": \"sk-ant-...\",\n      \"stored_at\": \"2026-11-14T12:00:00Z\"\n    },\n    \"openai\": {\n      \"key\": \"sk-...\",\n      \"stored_at\": \"2026-11-14T12:00:00.123Z\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- Written with 2-space indentation, top-level order `version`,\n  `providers`; provider names sorted ascending; entry order `key`,\n  `stored_at`; trailing newline. An empty store writes `\"providers\": {}`.\n- `stored_at` is written in UTC as RFC 3339 with `Z`, fractional seconds\n  present only when non-zero and with trailing zeros trimmed (Go's\n  `RFC3339Nano` form). JavaScript `Date` has millisecond precision, so at\n  most three fractional digits are produced; an existing entry's\n  `stored_at` string is preserved verbatim when the file is rewritten for\n  another provider.\n\n**Loading** (`loadCredentials(path)` -> `Map<provider, Entry>`):\n\n1. Open the file. `ENOENT` -> empty map (a missing file holds no\n   credentials). Other open error -> `read credentials: <cause>`.\n2. `fstat` the open descriptor (follows symlinks, like Go). If\n   `mode & 0o077 !== 0` (any group or other bit, not only read) -> error\n   `credentials file <path> is readable by other users (mode <perm>); run\n   `chmod 600 <path>``. Never skipped, never auto-fixed.\n3. Read the contents and decode. Decode errors are reported as\n   `credentials file <path>: <cause>`. Decoding rejects:\n   - not JSON, or trailing data after the top-level value;\n   - any unknown member at the top level or inside an entry;\n   - `version` missing or not the integer 1\n     (`unsupported version <v> (want 1)`);\n   - a provider name that `lookupProvider` does not know\n     (`unknown provider \"<name>\"`);\n   - an entry whose `key` is missing, not a string, or empty after\n     trimming whitespace (`provider \"<name>\" has an empty key`);\n   - `stored_at` present but not an RFC 3339 timestamp string.\n   Accepted: `providers` missing or `null` (empty map); `stored_at`\n   missing or `null` (treated as Go's zero time, written back as\n   `0001-01-01T00:00:00Z` if the file is rewritten).\n4. Load errors are not cached (2.13).\n\n**Saving** (`saveCredential(path, provider, key, storedAt)`):\n\n1. `provider` unknown -> `save credential: unknown provider \"<name>\"`.\n2. `key.trim() === \"\"` -> `save credential: empty key`.\n3. Load the existing file (2.12 Loading); any error aborts (so an\n   over-permissive or malformed file blocks saving).\n4. Set `providers[provider] = {key, stored_at: storedAt in UTC}`, replacing\n   any previous entry. The key is stored as given (not trimmed).\n5. Write atomically (below).\n\n**Deleting** (`deleteCredential(path, provider)`): load (errors abort); if\nthe provider has no entry, or the file is missing, do nothing (no write);\nelse remove it and write atomically. The file remains with the other\nproviders, or with an empty `providers` object.\n\n**Atomic write** for credentials:\n\n1. `mkdir -p` the parent with mode `0o700`.\n2. `chmod` the parent to `0o700` even if it already existed (tightens a\n   `0o755` `.git/roast` created by the trust record, or a loose\n   `~/.config/roast`).\n3. Create `credentials-<random>.json` in the parent with exclusive create\n   and mode `0o600`, write the bytes, close.\n4. Rename over `credentials.json`. Remove the temp file on any failure.\n5. Afterwards the directory holds only `credentials.json` (plus whatever\n   else was there before, such as `trust.json`); no temp file survives.\n6. Error prefix: `write credentials: <cause>`.\n\nNo `fsync` (Go parity).\n\n### 2.13 Credential store (the `StoredCredentials` implementation)\n\nConfig consumes stored credentials only through:\n\n```ts\ntype CredentialScope = 'project'|'user'\ninterface StoredCredentials {\n    // Returns null when neither scope holds a key for provider.\n    // Rejects when a credentials file it reaches cannot be used;\n    // never skips a bad file.\n    lookup(provider:string):Promise<{key:string, scope:CredentialScope}|null>\n}\n```\n\n`CredentialStore(dir, env)` implements it:\n\n- Lookup order: project scope, then user scope. The first scope whose map\n  has the provider wins, returning its key and scope name.\n- Files are read lazily: the project file is read on the first lookup;\n  the user file only when a lookup gets past the project scope without a\n  match. Each file is read at most once per store after a successful load\n  (cache the loaded map, or an in-flight promise, per scope; on failure\n  drop the cache entry so the next lookup retries and fails again).\n- An error loading the project file rejects the lookup even if the user\n  file holds the key.\n- `dir === \"\"`: no project scope. `projectCredentialsPath` failing (not a\n  repository): no project scope, cached. `userCredentialsPath` failing (no\n  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and no `HOME`): no user scope, cached.\n- The CLI constructs one store per command run with the working directory\n  and injected environment, and attaches it to every config it loads\n  (`withStoredCredentials`, config area). Any path that resolves roles from\n  a loaded config must attach it, or stored keys are silently unused.\n\nHow config uses it (restated from the config area for context): a stored\nkey is consulted only after `ROAST_<ROLE>_API_KEY`, the profile's\n`api_key_env`, and the provider's standard variable; only when\n`providerAt(role endpoint, role protocol)` finds a provider; and the\nprovider name passed to `lookup` comes from that registry match, never\nfrom anything in the project config. A rejected lookup fails role\nresolution, which `review` reports as a usage error (exit 2) before any\nnetwork request.\n\n### 2.14 Doctor (reference only; doctor area owns it)\n\n- `config-trust` check: pass `no approval required ...` when\n  `!requiresApproval`; fail when outside a Git repository, when the record\n  cannot be read (`approval required; could not read approvals: <err>`),\n  or when not approved (`... endpoint probes skipped; run `npx roast\n  trust``); pass when approved. In TS, a `'legacy'` (version 1) record is a\n  fail whose detail says `approved by an older Roast` and names\n  `npx roast trust`. Doctor never prompts and never approves.\n- When not trusted, doctor skips the endpoint and round-trip probes for\n  every role (the probes would send credentials to an unapproved\n  destination).\n- `project-credentials` / `user-credentials` checks: for each scope whose\n  path resolves and whose file exists (`lstat`), `loadCredentials`; fail\n  with the load error, else pass with `<path> (<sorted providers>)` or\n  `<path> (none stored)`. A broken file fails doctor even when environment\n  credentials shadow it.\n\n### 2.15 Security invariants enforced here\n\nFrom `docs/security-model.md`, ADR-0001, ADR-0002:\n\n1. No network request is made for a config that requires approval until\n   this clone has approved its exact projection hash (review fails closed;\n   doctor skips probes).\n2. Approval state lives only under the Git common directory, which `git\n   clone` never copies; a repository cannot ship its own approval.\n3. Only `y` / `yes` records approval; `--yes` is the only non-interactive\n   path. There is no environment-variable bypass.\n4. Every value shown for approval is escaped, so config text cannot alter\n   what the user is asked to approve.\n5. Any change to an endpoint, protocol, credential variable, or\n   prompt-override path invalidates the approval; other edits do not.\n6. Approvals recorded by the Go implementation are never honored.\n7. A malformed or unsupported trust record is an error, never read as\n   \"approved\" or silently replaced.\n8. A project config never holds a credential (config area rejects\n   `api_key`); stored keys come only from credentials files outside the\n   working tree.\n9. A stored key is matched to a role only through the built-in provider\n   registry (endpoint plus accepted protocol), so a custom endpoint, or a\n   role an override moved off its provider's endpoint, never receives one.\n10. Stored keys rank below every environment variable.\n11. Credentials files are created `0o600` in a `0o700` directory; a file\n    with any group/other permission bit stops resolution with an error\n    naming `chmod 600`, never skipped.\n12. A malformed credentials file (unknown provider or field, wrong\n    version, empty key) is an error, never skipped.\n13. Stored keys never appear in stdout, stderr, debug output, or run\n    records (record area enforces exclusion from metadata; this area never\n    logs a key or includes it in an error message).\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\n```\nsrc/config/trust.ts       projection, requiresApproval, hash v2\nsrc/config/canonical.ts   RFC 8785 serializer for string-only documents\nsrc/trust/trust.ts        stateDir, trustPath, record read/approve\nsrc/credentials/credentials.ts\n                          paths, load/save/delete, CredentialStore\nsrc/fsutil/atomic.ts      writeFileAtomic (shared with trust; other areas\n                          may reuse)\nsrc/cli/trust.ts          `trust` command, requireTrust, summary, prompt\nsrc/cli/prompt.ts         interactiveTerminals, line reader (shared)\n```\n\n`src/config/trust.ts` (pure, no I/O):\n\n```ts\nexport interface TrustedProfile { /* 2.2 */ }\nexport interface PromptOverride { /* 2.2 */ }\nexport interface TrustProjection { /* 2.2 */ }\nexport function trustProjection(cfg:Config):TrustProjection\nexport function requiresApproval(p:TrustProjection):boolean\nexport function trustHashDocument(p:TrustProjection):HashDocument\nexport function trustHash(p:TrustProjection):string  // \"sha256:<hex>\"\n```\n\n`trustHash` uses `node:crypto` `createHash('sha256')`. Depends on the\nconfig area's `Config`, `providerAt`.\n\n`src/config/canonical.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport type CanonicalValue =\n    string|CanonicalValue[]|{[key:string]:CanonicalValue}\nexport function canonicalJson(value:CanonicalValue):string\n```\n\nThrows on a string containing a lone surrogate (RFC 8785 requires\nwell-formed Unicode). This is a defensive backstop only: config\nvalidation rejects lone surrogates (config area, spec.md cross-cutting\nrules), so hashing never sees one.\n\n`src/config/config.ts` (config area) exports the `StoredCredentials`\ninterface (2.13) so config never imports `src/credentials/` (Go keeps the\nsame direction: credentials imports config for `lookupProvider`).\n\n`src/trust/trust.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport async function stateDir(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n    :Promise<string>\nexport async function trustPath(dir:string, signal?:AbortSignal)\n    :Promise<string>\nexport type TrustRecord =\n    {kind:'current', approved:string[]}|{kind:'legacy'}\nexport async function readTrustRecord(path:string):Promise<TrustRecord>\nexport type ApprovalState = 'approved'|'not-approved'|'legacy'\nexport async function isApproved(dir:string, hash:string)\n    :Promise<ApprovalState>\nexport async function approve(dir:string, hash:string):Promise<void>\n```\n\nGit runs through `execFile('git', [...], {cwd:dir})` (Git area helper).\n\n`src/credentials/credentials.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport const CREDENTIALS_FILE = 'credentials.json'\nexport type CredentialScope = 'project'|'user'\nexport interface Entry { key:string, storedAt:string }\nexport async function projectCredentialsPath(dir:string):Promise<string>\nexport function userCredentialsPath(\n    env:Record<string, string|undefined>):string\nexport async function loadCredentials(path:string)\n    :Promise<Map<string, Entry>>\nexport async function saveCredential(path:string, provider:string,\n    key:string, storedAt:Date):Promise<void>\nexport async function deleteCredential(path:string, provider:string)\n    :Promise<void>\nexport class CredentialStore implements StoredCredentials {\n    constructor(dir:string, env:Record<string, string|undefined>)\n    lookup(provider:string)\n        :Promise<{key:string, scope:CredentialScope}|null>\n}\n```\n\n`storedAt` is kept as the original string so rewrites preserve it. The\ndecoder is a `zod` strict schema plus the provider/key/version checks of\n2.12. Depends on `src/config` (`lookupProvider`), `src/trust`\n(`stateDir`), `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`.\n\n`src/fsutil/atomic.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport async function writeFileAtomic(path:string, data:string, opts:{\n    tempPrefix:string     // 'trust-' | 'credentials-'\n    dirMode:number        // 0o755 | 0o700\n    chmodDir:boolean      // false for trust, true for credentials\n    errorPrefix:string    // 'write trust record' | 'write credentials'\n}):Promise<void>\n```\n\nTemp name: `<tempPrefix><random hex>.json`, opened with flags `wx` and\nmode `0o600`.\n\n`src/cli/trust.ts`:\n\n```ts\nexport async function requireTrust(dir:string, cfg:Config,\n    io:CliIo):Promise<void>        // throws UsageError or Error\nexport function writeTrustSummary(out:Writable,\n    p:TrustProjection):void\nexport async function promptNoDefault(out:Writable,\n    lines:LineReader, question:string):Promise<boolean>\nexport function trustCommand(...):CommandModule  // yargs\n```\n\n`CliIo` is the in-process `run(argv, io)` bundle (stdin, stdout, stderr,\nenv, cwd, shared line reader). `UsageError` maps to exit 2; other errors\nto exit 4 (cli area). Debug namespace `roast:trust` and\n`roast:credentials`: may log paths, scope names, provider names, record\nversions, and hashes; never a key.\n\n## 4. Deliberate changes\n\n- **Trust hash v2** (decision 4, ADR-0003): SHA-256 over RFC 8785\n  canonical JSON of the hash document in 2.4, `trust.json` `version: 2`.\n  A version-1 record is never an approval: review fails as untrusted\n  (exit 2, message says approved by an older Roast, names\n  `npx roast trust`; on terminals the normal inline trust prompt is\n  offered, as for any untrusted config); approving\n  rewrites the file as version 2 with only the new hash. Differences from\n  the Go hash document beyond the algorithm: empty arrays serialize as\n  `[]` (Go wrote `null`), destination sort uses UTF-16 code units per\n  field instead of bytes of NUL-joined fields. The stored form stays\n  `sha256:<lowercase hex>`, and the test vectors in 2.4 are frozen.\n- **Credentials keys are case-sensitive** (deliberate change 7): Go\n  matched `credentials.json` field names case-insensitively\n  (`{\"Version\":1,\"Providers\":...}` loaded); TS matches exactly, so such a\n  file is rejected as an unknown member. Files Roast wrote are unaffected.\n- **Windows dropped** (decision 2): the Go `runtime.GOOS != \"windows\"`\n  guard on the permission check disappears; the check always runs.\n- **Debug tracing** (decision 5): `roast:trust`, `roast:credentials`; no\n  keys.\n\n## 5. Test plan\n\nSeams: CLI in-process (`run(argv, io)` with a `node:http` fake inference\nserver and swappable `fetch`) unless noted. Every CLI test runs in a temp\nGit repository and isolates `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in the injected\nenv. Assert exit codes, files, and requests received; never exact text,\nexcept that a message names `npx roast trust` (behavioral contract).\n\n### 5.1 Projection and approval need (unit, `src/config/trust.ts`)\n\n- Table of single-profile configs and whether they need approval: OpenAI\n  responses/chat with standard or no key var; implicit endpoint; implicit\n  protocol; trailing slash; Anthropic; OpenRouter chat/responses with\n  standard or no key; custom key var on each provider; another provider's\n  key var; mismatched protocol; gateway with and without key var\n  (TestStandardProfilesNeedNoApproval).\n- Projection order: roles sorted, author routes sorted after their role;\n  unselected profiles excluded; prompt overrides in fixed order (new).\n\n### 5.2 Hash v2 (unit)\n\n- Known-answer vectors 1 and 2 from 2.4 (new).\n- Duplicate destinations collapse; destination order independent of role\n  order; profile renames and selector changes keep the hash (new).\n- `canonicalJson` matches RFC 8785 on key ordering and string escaping,\n  including a non-ASCII key and a control character (new).\n- Destinations sort field by field in UTF-16 code unit order (a case with\n  an astral character versus a BMP character above U+E000); empty lists\n  hash as `[]` (new).\n- `canonicalJson` throws on a lone surrogate; the config area rejects one\n  before hashing (new).\n\n### 5.3 Trust record (unit, `src/trust/trust.ts`, temp Git repo)\n\n- Absent file: not approved; approve writes version 2 with one hash, mode\n  `0o600` (new).\n- Approve twice: one entry (new).\n- Version 1 file: `isApproved` returns `'legacy'`; approve replaces it\n  with version 2 `[hash]` (new).\n- Malformed JSON, `version: 3`, missing version, non-array `approved`:\n  `isApproved` and `approve` both throw; file unchanged (new).\n- Linked worktree resolves the same path (covered at CLI below).\n\n### 5.4 Review and the trust command (CLI)\n\n- Built-in-only config with author route reviews with no trust state\n  written (TestDefaultEndpointConfigNeedsNoApproval).\n- Untrusted config: exit neither 0 nor 1, message names `npx roast trust`,\n  zero requests (TestUntrustedConfigFailsClosedBeforeAnyRequest).\n- Summary names every profile, endpoint, protocol, credential var, prompt\n  path, and asks `[y/N]` (TestTrustSummaryNamesEverySecurityRelevantValue;\n  assert presence of config values only).\n- Control characters in profile names, route families, and prompt paths\n  never reach stdout raw; no config value starts its own line\n  (TestTrustSummaryEscapesControlCharacters).\n- `trust` records only on `y`/`Y`/`yes`/`y` without newline; `\"\"`, `\\n`,\n  `n`, `no`, `yy`, `sure` exit non-zero, record nothing, and the next\n  review still fails closed (TestTrustRecordsApprovalOnlyOnYes).\n- `trust --yes` never reads stdin and enables the review\n  (TestTrustYesRecordsWithoutReadingStdin).\n- Model, threshold, limit, and gate edits keep approval\n  (TestNonSecurityEditsKeepApproval).\n- Endpoint, routed endpoint, protocol, credential var, and prompt path\n  edits require re-approval, and re-approval works\n  (TestSecurityEditsRequireApprovalAgain).\n- Approval in the main worktree is honored in a linked worktree, and not\n  before (TestApprovalIsSharedWithLinkedWorktrees).\n- Inline prompt on simulated terminals: `y` approves, summary on stderr,\n  record written, later non-terminal review passes\n  (TestInlineTrustPromptApprovesOnYes).\n- Inline decline (`\"\"`, `\\n`, `n`, `no`, `yy`): asks `[y/N]`, no requests,\n  no record (TestInlineTrustPromptDeclinedSendsNothing).\n- Non-terminal review checks for terminals, never reads stdin, never\n  prompts, names `npx roast trust`\n  (TestNonTerminalReviewFailsClosedWithoutPrompting).\n- Default terminal detection rejects non-TTY streams\n  (TestDefaultTerminalDetectionRejectsNonFiles; unit).\n- Legacy record: review exits 2 with zero requests; `trust --yes` then\n  makes it pass and the file is version 2; on simulated terminals `y`\n  approves inline (new).\n- Malformed record: review exits 4 with zero requests (new, Go parity;\n  resolved in spec.md \"Kept as Go does\").\n\n### 5.5 Credentials (unit, `src/credentials/credentials.ts`)\n\n- Project path is `<git-common-dir>/roast/credentials.json`\n  (TestProjectPathIsInsideTheGitCommonDir).\n- User path follows `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, else `HOME/.config`\n  (TestUserPathFollowsXDGConfigHome).\n- Save writes `0o600` in `0o700`, replaces atomically, leaves only the\n  file, round-trips keys and `stored_at`\n  (TestSaveWritesAPrivateFileAndReplacesItAtomically).\n- Save tightens an existing `0o755` directory\n  (TestSaveTightensAnExistingDirectory).\n- Delete removes one provider; deleting from a missing file is a no-op\n  (TestDeleteRemovesOneProvider).\n- Missing file loads empty (TestLoadMissingFileIsEmpty).\n- Rejects unknown provider, unknown entry field, unknown top-level field,\n  missing version, wrong version, empty key, non-JSON\n  (TestLoadRejectsMalformedFiles).\n- Member names match exactly: `{\"Version\":1,...}` and `\"Providers\"` or\n  `\"Key\"` spellings are rejected as unknown members (new, deliberate\n  change 7).\n- Rejects modes `0o640`, `0o604`, `0o644`\n  (TestLoadRejectsAFileOthersCanRead); add `0o620` (any group/other bit).\n- Store prefers project over user scope\n  (TestStorePrefersProjectScopeOverUserScope).\n- Outside a repository only user scope is used\n  (TestStoreOutsideARepositoryUsesOnlyUserScope).\n- A bad file rejects the lookup (TestStoreReportsABadFile).\n\n### 5.6 Stored credentials through review and doctor (CLI)\n\n- Project stored key reaches the fake Anthropic endpoint\n  (TestReviewSendsAProjectStoredKey).\n- Env beats project beats user (TestEnvironmentBeatsProjectBeatsUserStoredKeys).\n- Stored key never reaches a custom endpoint\n  (TestStoredKeyNeverReachesACustomEndpoint).\n- Stored key does not follow an endpoint override\n  (TestStoredKeyDoesNotFollowAnEndpointOverride).\n- An over-permissive file in either scope fails the review with zero\n  requests (TestACredentialsFileOthersCanReadFailsTheReview).\n- No copy of the stored key in stdout, stderr, or any run-record file\n  (TestReviewLeavesNoCopyOfAStoredKey).\n- Doctor reports a stored credential and its scope; fails a broken file;\n  reports a usable file; still runs other checks with a broken file\n  (TestDoctorReportsAStoredCredentialAndItsScope,\n  TestDoctorFailsABrokenCredentialsFile,\n  TestDoctorReportsAUsableCredentialsFile,\n  TestDoctorRunsItsOtherChecksWithABrokenCredentialsFile; doctor area).\n\n## 6. Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n","numLines":470,"startLine":404,"totalLines":911}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01L1y5rv2T9bka4qMD6XomPQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
